WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.270 --> 00:00:02.730
- [Narrator] An economic crisis is looming

00:00:02.730 --> 00:00:06.060
and experts are sounding
the alarm, afraid of-

00:00:06.060 --> 00:00:08.220
- Self-imposed calamity.

00:00:08.220 --> 00:00:10.140
- [Narrator] Treasury
Secretary Janet Yellen

00:00:10.140 --> 00:00:13.200
is talking about the dispute
over raising the debt ceiling,

00:00:13.200 --> 00:00:14.820
which hasn't happened yet.

00:00:14.820 --> 00:00:18.090
- Can't be negotiated over whether or not

00:00:18.090 --> 00:00:20.250
we're going to pay our bills.

00:00:20.250 --> 00:00:22.590
- [Narrator] But as the
countdown to potential calamity

00:00:22.590 --> 00:00:24.120
ticks closer to zero,

00:00:24.120 --> 00:00:27.300
the US Treasury is
buying America more time

00:00:27.300 --> 00:00:29.910
by taking so-called extraordinary measures

00:00:29.910 --> 00:00:32.610
because if the government
hits its borrowing limit-

00:00:32.610 --> 00:00:34.710
- You have a real mess on your hands.

00:00:34.710 --> 00:00:37.200
- [Narrator] So what are
these extraordinary measures?

00:00:37.200 --> 00:00:39.450
How do they help avoid a crisis?

00:00:39.450 --> 00:00:42.630
And what happens when they've run out?

00:00:42.630 --> 00:00:44.730
First, it's important to understand

00:00:44.730 --> 00:00:47.160
what makes them extraordinary.

00:00:47.160 --> 00:00:49.050
The Treasury pays the country's bills

00:00:49.050 --> 00:00:50.970
by using revenue from taxes,

00:00:50.970 --> 00:00:53.190
because it spends more than it taxes,

00:00:53.190 --> 00:00:55.470
it makes up the difference by issuing debt

00:00:55.470 --> 00:00:57.450
in the form of government securities.

00:00:57.450 --> 00:00:59.940
But there is a self-imposed
congressional limit

00:00:59.940 --> 00:01:02.640
to how much of this debt
the department can issue.

00:01:02.640 --> 00:01:03.870
That's the ceiling.

00:01:03.870 --> 00:01:07.890
- The only way to fund a
deficit is to borrow more money

00:01:07.890 --> 00:01:10.290
and until it gets rid of the deficit

00:01:10.290 --> 00:01:12.690
that debt is going to keep rising.

00:01:12.690 --> 00:01:15.090
- [Narrator] It's on Congress
to raise that ceiling

00:01:15.090 --> 00:01:17.610
to allow the Treasury to issue more debt,

00:01:17.610 --> 00:01:21.060
and cover spending that
lawmakers already approved,

00:01:21.060 --> 00:01:24.330
but there's a cap on how much
debt the department can issue,

00:01:24.330 --> 00:01:25.163
that limit-

00:01:25.163 --> 00:01:25.996
- The debt ceiling.

00:01:25.996 --> 00:01:26.829
- The debt ceiling.

00:01:26.829 --> 00:01:27.662
- The debt ceiling.

00:01:27.662 --> 00:01:29.490
- If all this sudden
overnight you can't borrow

00:01:29.490 --> 00:01:30.570
any more money,

00:01:30.570 --> 00:01:33.090
well, then you start missing
payments that you've promised.

00:01:33.090 --> 00:01:34.740
- [Narrator] This is where
the Treasury comes in

00:01:34.740 --> 00:01:36.330
with its extraordinary measures

00:01:36.330 --> 00:01:39.300
to prevent the country from
reaching the debt ceiling.

00:01:39.300 --> 00:01:41.910
- The Treasury uses these
extraordinary measures

00:01:41.910 --> 00:01:43.440
because it's got no alternative.

00:01:43.440 --> 00:01:44.760
If it can't borrow more money,

00:01:44.760 --> 00:01:46.500
it's gotta find the money somewhere.

00:01:46.500 --> 00:01:48.540
- [Narrator] So the Treasury
accesses other funds

00:01:48.540 --> 00:01:51.060
it has tucked away,
pulling money from one area

00:01:51.060 --> 00:01:54.210
to use for more pressing
spending needs in another.

00:01:54.210 --> 00:01:58.110
- Imagine that you have an
envelope hidden away in a drawer

00:01:58.110 --> 00:02:00.570
with a bunch of cash in
it to deal with a crisis,

00:02:00.570 --> 00:02:01.860
this is the envelope.

00:02:01.860 --> 00:02:04.530
- [Narrator] But how does
the Treasury get that cash?

00:02:04.530 --> 00:02:07.170
One way is by using
money it has stowed away

00:02:07.170 --> 00:02:09.180
for its own retirees.

00:02:09.180 --> 00:02:11.790
On January 19th, Yellen
announced the Treasury

00:02:11.790 --> 00:02:13.140
would be redeeming investments

00:02:13.140 --> 00:02:15.720
from the Civil Service
Retirement and Disability Fund

00:02:15.720 --> 00:02:18.960
and the Postal Service
Retiree Health Benefits Fund.

00:02:18.960 --> 00:02:21.135
They're one of the tools in the envelope.

00:02:21.135 --> 00:02:23.970
Pulling investments doesn't
shut down these funds

00:02:23.970 --> 00:02:26.820
or mean that retirees
won't get their payments.

00:02:26.820 --> 00:02:29.760
It does mean that these
funds will be tapped now

00:02:29.760 --> 00:02:32.160
while extraordinary measures continue.

00:02:32.160 --> 00:02:34.980
Another way is by pausing investments.

00:02:34.980 --> 00:02:37.830
On January 23rd, Yellen paused investments

00:02:37.830 --> 00:02:39.990
in the Government
Securities Investment Fund,

00:02:39.990 --> 00:02:43.710
which is invested in short
term US Treasury securities.

00:02:43.710 --> 00:02:46.140
In addition, the Treasury
can pause payments

00:02:46.140 --> 00:02:47.070
to government funds

00:02:47.070 --> 00:02:49.830
that don't have to
immediately pay beneficiaries.

00:02:49.830 --> 00:02:52.740
But the Treasury doesn't
just take and pause

00:02:52.740 --> 00:02:54.900
without refilling the envelope.

00:02:54.900 --> 00:02:57.420
- Typically, after the
debt ceiling is increased,

00:02:57.420 --> 00:02:59.340
they'll borrow money from the the public,

00:02:59.340 --> 00:03:00.690
from bond investors,

00:03:00.690 --> 00:03:02.850
and then replenish those pension accounts

00:03:02.850 --> 00:03:04.020
that it's taken from.

00:03:04.020 --> 00:03:06.360
- [Narrator] However, the
Treasury's extraordinary measures

00:03:06.360 --> 00:03:09.540
are temporary meant only
to tie the government over

00:03:09.540 --> 00:03:11.550
until the debt ceiling is raised.

00:03:11.550 --> 00:03:12.510
- The end of the day,

00:03:12.510 --> 00:03:15.690
Congress simply has to act
to raise the debt ceiling.

00:03:15.690 --> 00:03:17.640
- House Republicans on the other hand

00:03:17.640 --> 00:03:20.850
want to use the debt ceiling
as leverage to force a debate

00:03:20.850 --> 00:03:24.270
with Democrats about cutting
spending to curb future debt.

00:03:24.270 --> 00:03:27.600
- It doesn't matter if you sit
before a four star general,

00:03:27.600 --> 00:03:30.030
they'll tell you the debt
is the greatest threat

00:03:30.030 --> 00:03:30.960
to this nation.

00:03:30.960 --> 00:03:31.793
- [Narrator] This has become

00:03:31.793 --> 00:03:33.960
something of a recurring political fight.

00:03:33.960 --> 00:03:38.130
- Let's pay our bills,
not torch our economy.

00:03:38.130 --> 00:03:41.280
- In the past, they've always
raised the debt ceiling

00:03:41.280 --> 00:03:43.650
and tried to get back to
some state of normalcy.

00:03:43.650 --> 00:03:45.570
If they run out of funds

00:03:45.570 --> 00:03:47.880
in using these extraordinary measures,

00:03:47.880 --> 00:03:50.070
and they haven't run the debt ceiling,

00:03:50.070 --> 00:03:51.390
then they don't pay their bills.

00:03:51.390 --> 00:03:53.190
- [Narrator] Yellen said
that she expected them

00:03:53.190 --> 00:03:55.500
to keep the government from
hitting the debt ceiling

00:03:55.500 --> 00:03:57.060
until early June.

00:03:57.060 --> 00:03:59.430
If the ceiling isn't
raised and the Treasury

00:03:59.430 --> 00:04:03.180
can't meet all of its
obligations, the US defaults.

00:04:03.180 --> 00:04:04.380
It's called the X-date,

00:04:04.380 --> 00:04:07.020
and it's never happened
in American history.

00:04:07.020 --> 00:04:09.180
So what would happen?

00:04:09.180 --> 00:04:11.040
- Bond holders don't get paid interest,

00:04:11.040 --> 00:04:14.910
retirees don't get paid
social security funds,

00:04:14.910 --> 00:04:18.540
contractors don't get paid
for military spending,

00:04:18.540 --> 00:04:20.520
and then you've got a
real mess on your hands.

00:04:20.520 --> 00:04:22.740
- [Narrator] That mess
could cause a disaster

00:04:22.740 --> 00:04:24.390
in financial markets.

00:04:24.390 --> 00:04:28.450
- It is possible for markets
to become quite concerned

00:04:29.400 --> 00:04:33.390
about whether or not the
US will pay its bills

00:04:33.390 --> 00:04:35.730
before the day of reckoning comes.

00:04:35.730 --> 00:04:36.750
- [Narrator] That could even mean

00:04:36.750 --> 00:04:39.030
a possible credit downgrade.

00:04:39.030 --> 00:04:40.800
- One of the last times
we had a moment like this

00:04:40.800 --> 00:04:44.640
was Standard &amp; Poor's
downgraded the AAA credit rating

00:04:44.640 --> 00:04:46.710
on US Treasury securities.

00:04:46.710 --> 00:04:48.900
- After witnessing a month of wrangling

00:04:48.900 --> 00:04:51.090
over raising the debt ceiling,

00:04:51.090 --> 00:04:54.210
they doubted our political
systems' ability to act.

00:04:54.210 --> 00:04:57.000
- There are real consequences
if we get to a moment

00:04:57.000 --> 00:04:59.610
where extraordinary measures are exhausted

00:04:59.610 --> 00:05:01.560
and the US has no way

00:05:01.560 --> 00:05:04.140
to continue to fund its budget deficits.

00:05:04.140 --> 00:05:06.810
- [Narrator] The Treasury says
the US won't reach the X-date

00:05:06.810 --> 00:05:08.040
until this summer.

00:05:08.040 --> 00:05:10.200
In the meantime, the
Treasury will continue

00:05:10.200 --> 00:05:12.360
implementing extraordinary measures

00:05:12.360 --> 00:05:14.340
until the debt ceiling is raised

00:05:14.340 --> 00:05:17.012
or that emergency envelope is empty.

00:05:17.012 --> 00:05:19.595
(upbeat music)

